# Tunables for llama-server-limited.service - reduced VRAM footprint, for
# sharing the GPU with a desktop session.
#
# Installed by install.sh to ~/.config/llama-server/limited.env and passed
# to llm-server.sh via --tunables-file by systemd/llama-server-limited.service.
# Edit values here and `systemctl --user restart llama-server-limited.service`
# to pick them up - no need to touch the .service file or llm-server.sh.
#
# Anything left commented uses llm-server.sh's built-in default for that
# value. Uncomment and edit any line to override it. Full explanations of
# each value: ../scripts/llm-server.sh --help

# --- values that define this deployment (uncommented for visibility) ---
LLAMA_NGL=99
# 33, not 38: a 12GB card running a normal desktop session still has room to
# keep 5 more MoE layers on the GPU. Fewer CPU-resident layers means less host
# RAM and faster token generation, and the memory ceilings in
# systemd/llama-server-limited.service are sized against this value - raising
# it pushes more weight into host RAM than those caps assume.
LLAMA_NCMOE=33
LLAMA_THREADS=8
LLAMA_CTX_SIZE=131072

# --- memory stability (uncommented - see README's "Memory stability" section) ---
# ctx-checkpoints is 8 here, LOWER than the other two profiles' 32 (which is
# llama-server's own default) - and deliberately not 0. It was previously 0
# on the belief that checkpoints never restore on this architecture; that
# was WRONG and cost real performance. Measured on llama.cpp b10087,
# checkpoints are what lets the prompt cache below actually restore a
# branch: at 0, returning to a cached branch reprocessed the entire prompt
# (11-14k tokens, 13-16s); at any non-zero value the same switch cost 25
# tokens and 0.3s. See README's "Memory stability" section.
#
# 8 rather than 32 because this is the desktop-sharing profile and 8 is a
# tighter ceiling on worst-case growth. That costs nothing measurable: 4, 8
# and 32 were identical in both benefit and memory on a 2-branch workload,
# and 2 branches is exactly this profile's documented scope (see cache-ram
# below). Checkpoints are allocated on demand, so this is a ceiling, not a
# reservation. Raise toward 32 if this profile ever runs busier sessions.
#
# cache-ram is 5120, raised from 3072 on 2026-07-25 after measuring against a
# NEAR-FULL context rather than a synthetic multi-branch test. The old value
# was derived from "~11 KiB/token, so a max-128k branch costs ~1.4GiB, and 2
# branches fit in 3072". That per-token figure is right for attention KV but
# badly understates a real cache entry, which also carries checkpoint and
# slot state:
#
#   measured entry size    ~88k-token branch  -> 2896 MiB
#                          ~125k-token branch -> ~4 GiB (exceeds 3072 entirely)
#
# At 3072 a single ~125k-token branch could not be cached AT ALL, so every
# switch back to it reprocessed the whole prompt: 125702 tokens, 190.25s. At
# 5120 the identical switch cost 5 tokens and 0.32s - ~594x - with zero
# evictions and no increase in peak memory.
#
# Note this is a CAP, not a reservation (see README's Memory stability
# section): raising it costs nothing until a workload actually uses it, and
# the real limiting factor is MemoryMax in the systemd unit. The failure mode
# at too-low values is abrupt, not gradual - the cache does not degrade, it
# stops working once one entry no longer fits.
#
# Budget for more than 2 branches: sequential *agents* is not the same as 2
# total prefixes. A coding client also fires title generation concurrently
# with the first message of every session, plus auto-summarization near the
# context limit - neither is an agent, both are extra branches.
LLAMA_CACHE_RAM=5120
LLAMA_CTX_CHECKPOINTS=8

# --- everything else (commented = using llm-server.sh's built-in default) ---
#LLAMA_MODEL_PATH="$HOME/llama-server/models/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MTP-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf"
#LLAMA_PARALLEL=1                # must stay 1 while LLAMA_SPEC_TYPE=draft-mtp
#LLAMA_CACHE_TYPE_K=q8_0
#LLAMA_CACHE_TYPE_V=q8_0
#LLAMA_SPEC_TYPE=draft-mtp
#LLAMA_SPEC_DRAFT_N_MAX=4        # tuned by testing - falls off sharply above 4
# Sampling below is a per-request DEFAULT, not deployment policy: any API
# client sending its own sampler block overrides it, and coding agents send
# one on every call (OpenCode sends temperature/top_p/top_k/presence_penalty
# /min_p/repeat_penalty each time). These reach raw curl and silent clients
# only - tune an agent's sampling in that agent's config. Verify with /slots,
# not /props (/props shows only the server's CLI-baked baseline).
#LLAMA_TEMP=1.0
#LLAMA_TOP_P=0.95
#LLAMA_TOP_K=20
#LLAMA_PRESENCE_PENALTY=1.5
#LLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0
#LLAMA_PORT=8080
#LLAMA_NO_MMAP=1                 # 1 = pass --no-mmap, 0 = allow mmap
#LLAMA_FLASH_ATTN=on
#LLAMA_EXTRA_ARGS=""
